.TH VIGILANCE 1 "" "vigilance" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vigilance \- edge framework for what a machine does when you step away
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vigilant go
.RB { open | lock | sleep | suspend }
.RI [ atleast | atmost ]
.br
.B vigilant force
.IR state ", " "vigilant only " state
.br
.B vigilant
.RB { status | verify | hooks | report | rescue }
.br
.B vigilant plan
.RI [ edge ]
.br
.B vigilant
.RB { due " [" edge ] | enforce | audit }
.br
.B vigilant timer
.RI [ reset ]
.br
.B swayidle-mgr ", " idle-capture ", " lock-watch
.SH DESCRIPTION
vigilance is an edge framework. It owns a LADDER of states, the ordering and
state discipline of the transitions between them, failure reporting, and
verification. It owns no specific hardware and no specific compositor: those
are plugins.
.PP
The name is the charter. Two pillars: make sure all the things go dark when
they should, and make sure the mechanism that is supposed to do it is actually
working. The second is why vigilance SUPERVISES the stock tools rather than
trusting them or patching them.
.SH THE LADDER
One dimension, how far the machine has withdrawn. Present tense throughout, so
a state names a condition the machine is IN:
.TP
.B open
Fully here; unlocked and lit.
.TP
.B lock
Session secured; the screen is still lit.
.TP
.B sleep
Peripherals down (DDC standby, backlights 0, LEDs off).
.TP
.B suspend
Machine in S3 or hibernate.
.PP
.B go
is declarative and traverses: say where the machine should BE and every edge in
between is crossed.
.B only
crosses exactly one edge.
.B force
ignores the recorded depth, for when reality has drifted from the record.
.PP
Being declarative,
.B go
travels in EITHER direction. That is correct for a resume, since
.B "go lock"
from
.B sleep
is how a woken machine gets its screen back, and wrong for a lock request,
which asks only that the session be SECURED. The
.B atleast
qualifier is that second sense: it deepens toward the state and never raises
toward it, so a machine already at
.B sleep
stays dark. Use it wherever the intent is security rather than placement.
Answering a lid\-close with the plain verb lit the panel and left it lit.
.PP
.B atmost
is its mirror, for a RESTORE gesture: it raises toward the state and never
descends toward it, so a key meaning "turn the display back on" brings a dark
machine up to
.B lock
and does nothing at all when the screen is already lit. Given the plain verb
such a key LOCKS a machine in use, because
.B go
travels whichever way reaches the target. Use
.B atleast
where the intent is to secure, and
.B atmost
where it is to restore.
.PP
.B "vigilant go suspend"
does NOT suspend. It fires that edge's hooks, invoked BY the unit ordered
Before=sleep.target. systemd stays the actuator; vigilant only crosses edges.
.SH EDGES
A descent edge shares its name with the state it enters
.RB ( lock ", " sleep ", " suspend );
the ascent needs its own words
.RB ( unlock ", " wake ", " resume ).
Ascent is not the descent reversed: leaving
.B sleep
the saved state is valid, so a hook restores it; leaving
.B suspend
USB has re-enumerated and the saved state is stale, so the hook re-asserts.
.SH THE THREE QUESTIONS
Supervision asks three things that differ in TENSE, and no one of them can
answer another. Each is a separate hook kind for that reason.
.TP
.B verify
NOW: does the hardware match the rung we believe we are at?
.TP
.B due
SOON: is an edge pending and past its declared deadline?
.TP
.B audit
PAST: did an event that already happened produce the edge it should have?
.PP
The third is the only one that can see an event which produced NO edge. A unit
that dies on a bad
.B ExecStart
leaves nothing in a wrong state and nothing pending, so only history shows it.
.PP
.B enforce
is the loop a timer runs: it asks
.B due
whether an edge is overdue, asks the
.I block
tier whether something is legitimately preventing it, and then alerts. It acts
only when an edge is DEMONSTRABLY overdue: never on a tie, so the primary
mechanism wins the normal race and vigilant stays the backstop rather than a
competitor.
.PP
An IDLE-ANCHORED deadline is reported but never enforced. A
.I due
hook may print an anchor alongside its seconds, and
.B idle
means the clock starts at the last input, which vigilant cannot observe on
this stack. Measured from rung entry instead, it called
.B lock
overdue on a machine being actively typed on.
The forensic tier is the right home for those edges, since
.B audit
needs no idle clock at all. It only covers them if an
.I audit.d
source for them is WIRED, though, and none ships: the one shipped source reads
logind SLEEP events. An idle-anchored edge with no audit source is watched by
nothing, and
.B due
and
.B enforce
both say so explicitly rather than implying coverage that does not exist.
.SH THE IDLE INHIBIT BOUND
A held logind idle inhibitor legitimately suppresses every idle timeout, so an
idle-anchored deadline can pass without its edge firing and nothing is wrong:
that is a video call asking not to be interrupted, and
.B swayidle-mgr
arms a logind event precisely so the request is honoured.
.PP
Unbounded, it is also how a LEAKED inhibitor keeps a machine unlocked
indefinitely with nothing saying so. So the hold is measured, and there are two
bounds with a deliberate gap between them:
.TP
under
.B VIGILANCE_INHIBIT_REPORT
the deadline is deferred silently. A call is not a fault. The hold's start and
end are each logged once, so the record exists without a line a minute.
.TP
past it
an alert names the duration and, where logind can say so, the process HOLDING
the inhibitor, because the remedy is at that process.
.TP
past
.B VIGILANCE_INHIBIT_FORCE
the session is secured anyway:
.B "go lock atleast"
with
.BR VIGILANCE_SOURCE=inhibit-bound ,
so the crossing is attributable in the log.
.PP
.B "vigilant timer"
shows what is counting and how long is left: whether an inhibitor is held and
by what, the time to each bound, and the next edge's deadline. It says FIRST
whether a supervision pass has run recently, because nothing here counts on its
own and a frozen number printed as a countdown would mislead in the reassuring
direction.
.PP
.B "vigilant timer reset"
answers the report: it restarts the escalation clock without ending the hold,
so the next report comes a report-bound later and the forced lock a force-bound
later. That is what makes the force safe rather than a blind timer: reaching it
needs a notification ignored for the whole gap between the bounds AND a seat
genuinely idle past the edge's own deadline, so a call somebody is attending is
never cut off. A reset taken BEFORE a hold begins is a no-op, because the clock
starts at the hold; it exits 75 and says so.
.PP
The verb was called
.B ack
for a few hours. That named the gesture rather than the subject, so it could
not tell a reader what it would do;
.B ack
now refuses and points here, because a notification delivered before the rename
names it and a toast can sit in a tray for days.
.PP
Only the
.B lock
edge is ever forced. A dark rung is reported and never acted on, for the reason
.B suspend
is excluded entirely: nothing may enter a dark state on a timer, because that
arms no way back.
.PP
Setting either knob to 0 disables that half, so "report but never act" and
"trust the inhibitor completely" are both reachable as configuration.
.PP
The hold is read from logind, because logind is what the idle timer on this
stack honours: the question is not whether anything anywhere asked for
idleness to be suppressed, but whether the thing that suppresses THIS timer is
held. An idle daemon honouring
.B org.freedesktop.ScreenSaver.Inhibit
instead, which is the common route on an X11 desktop, holds inhibitors
vigilance cannot see here; the deferral then does not happen and the deadline
is reported overdue exactly as it was before, which is the safe direction and
still a gap for that integration.
.SH RESCUE
.B "vigilant rescue"
is meant for a bare, unmodified key, pressable BLIND when the screen is gone. It
does two things in this order, and the order is the point: RECORD first (by
calling
.BR report ,
so the evidence is exactly what report would have shown), then recover with
.BR "force open" .
The fault it exists for is intermittent and never happens while instrumented, so
the button IS the instrument; recovering first would burn the only evidence.
.PP
It actuates nothing itself. Anything it should drive belongs in a hook, where it
is one implementation instead of two.
.SH HOOKS
Seven kinds, in TWO SCOPES:
.TP
.I /etc/vigilance/hooks
MACHINE scope, root-owned, runs for every session including a greeter's.
Peripherals belong to the machine.
.TP
.I ~/.config/vigilance/hooks
USER scope, no privilege needed. Locking, audio and notifications belong to a
user.
.PP
A greeter session needs no configuration of its own: it simply has no user root,
so it gets the machine hooks and nothing else. The machine scope is the LOWER
layer, so it comes up first and goes down last, exactly as systemd orders units:
descent runs user 10-90 then machine 10-90, and ascent reverses both.
.SS The idle clock, and the deadline nobody could measure
The deadline that matters \- "the screen should have locked by now" \- is
expressed in IDLE time, and vigilant could not read idle time. Its only clock
was time\-since\-entering\-the\-rung, which OVER\-reports on a machine in use:
measured from rung entry it once called
.B lock
1616s overdue while the machine was being typed on.
.PP
So an idle anchor was refused outright, and refusing it made the SOON question
unanswerable, which left a whole tier inert. Three layers of machinery resting
on one missing number.
.PP
.I idle.d
is that number. It is CROSS\-CUTTING, like
.IR audit.d " and " alert.d ,
because idle time is a property of the SEAT rather than of any edge: several
deadlines on different edges all read one clock. A source prints seconds since
last input and exits 0, or exits 78 for "I cannot tell".
.PP
78 IS NOT 0 HERE, and the distinction is sharper than usual: 0 means "input one
second ago", so reading a missing answer as a number would silently reset every
deadline forever and report a perfectly healthy machine. When several sources
answer, the MINIMUM wins \- the one that saw input most recently is the one
that stops a stale source manufacturing a false overdue against a machine
somebody is sitting at.
.PP
.B report
carries a
.B deadlines
section saying, per deadline, whether anything can measure it. A deadline
nobody can measure is not a deadline, it is a sentence in a config file.
.SS What forcing was, and why it is gone
.B enforce
once had an acting half behind
.BR VIGILANCE_ENFORCE=force .
It is RETIRED (2026\-09\-20) and the knob does nothing.
.PP
It was unreachable by construction: only two edges are enforcement targets,
.B sleep
is refused outright because a forced descent into a dark rung arms no way back,
and
.B lock
was refused because its deadline is idle\-anchored. The intersection of
forceable and measurable was empty, so it was tested code that could never run
in production.
.PP
It had also been superseded without anyone noticing. Every failure it would
have acted on is now caught closer to the cause: a misarmed idle timer by
.B report
reading
.IR /proc/<pid>/cmdline ,
a failing provider by hook\-failure alerting, a lock that did not take by
.B verify
and
.IR locker\-up ,
continuous drift by the standing recheck, and the past by
.BR audit .
.PP
What remained uncovered was ONE sliver, and it is the failure that created this
package: the idle timer alive, correctly armed, and silently not firing. A
wedged timer passes the argv check, logs no event for
.B audit
to reconcile, and leaves the machine at a rung it genuinely matches. That wants
DETECTING, not forcing \- and detecting it is exactly what the idle clock makes
possible. Forcing would have added an actuator on the security path driven by a
measurement, for nothing the alert does not already give.
.SS Coverage: edges that act and cannot be checked
.B vigilant report
carries a
.B coverage
section naming every edge that has actuators and NO verify tier. It is asked
STATICALLY, for all edges, and that is what makes it able to see one the
machine is not currently on:
.B verify
and the standing recheck only ever ask about the rung we are at NOW, so an edge
with no verifier stays invisible until the machine happens to be there.
.PP
Found live on both boxes:
.B resume
fires four actuators, has no verify tier, and
.B "vigilant verify resume"
reported success. After an S3 cycle is the one moment nobody is watching, and
it is an edge with a history of failing.
.PP
A verifier in EITHER scope covers an edge, because the two scopes are one hook
set when an edge runs. Only EXECUTABLE hooks count, matching the runner
exactly: a hook that lost its mode bit in a copy never runs, and counting it
would certify an edge as checked that nothing checks. It WARNS rather than
fails, being a wiring gap and not a machine fault.
.SS plan: what runs, as whom
.B vigilant hooks
lists what is wired. It lists it for THIS session, implicitly, and in one fixed
order, and both of those silences have cost an outage.
.B vigilant plan
answers the question an integrator actually has: if this edge fires, in this
context, as that uid, what runs and in what order. It names three things a
merged listing cannot.
.TP
.B SCOPE
A hook wired in USER scope does not exist for the greeter at all. Each tier is
printed twice, once per context, and the user-only hooks are listed again under
.B "absent :"
so the shortfall is stated rather than inferred.
.TP
.B ORDER
The traversal REVERSES on an ascent, so the listing is built per edge from that
edge's own direction. A restore does not run in the order a descent did.
.TP
.B REACHABILITY
.I _hooks_in
lists a hook only if it is executable, and that test is FALSE for a uid which
cannot traverse to the target. A machine-scope hook pointing into a 0750 home
therefore does not FAIL for the greeter, it does not EXIST: no log, no alert,
no missing-hook warning, and the greeter's hook set is quietly smaller than the
wiring declares. That one is silent BY CONSTRUCTION, so it is the only thing
here that sets a non-zero exit.
.PP
Reachability is judged for
.BR VIGILANCE_GREETER_USER " (default " _greetd )
by walking EVERY component of the resolved target, because one non-traversable
directory hides what is under it as completely as a non-executable file does.
A named POSIX ACL entry granting that user traversal counts: reading the mode
bits alone would report a deliberate, correct grant as a fault. Where no such
account exists the verb says reachability was not checked rather than implying
it was clean. It runs no hook, crosses no edge and writes no log record.
.PP
vigilant hardcodes no mechanism in any kind.
.TP
.IB <edge> .d
DO it: actuators that drive the hardware.
.TP
.IB <edge> .verify.d
CHECK it: did the edge actually take effect.
.TP
.IB <edge> .report.d
TELL: announce the change outward (logind's SetLockedHint, a bar).
.TP
.IB <edge> .due.d
WHEN: when should this edge have fired? The timeout lives outside vigilant.
.TP
.IB <edge> .block.d
WHY NOT: is something legitimately preventing it? Exit 10 blocks, anything
else allows. Fails OPEN: a broken guard must never suppress a lock.
DESCENT EDGES ONLY. A block may refuse to take the machine down but never to
bring it back up: honouring one on
.BR unlock ", " wake " or " resume
would leave the machine in a dark rung with its only way out vetoed, and
disabled
.B vigilant rescue
outright. Such a hook is ignored and reported by
.B vigilant report
as a wiring error.
.TP
.I audit.d
DID IT: read an external event source and report events that SHOULD have
produced an edge. Prints
.I "<epoch> <edge> [note]"
per event; vigilant owns the reconciliation against its own log. NOT per-edge,
like
.IR alert.d :
the hook names the edge in its output, because one source reports events for
several edges.
.TP
.I alert.d
SHOUT: tell the human. Not per-edge; raised by vigilant itself.
.PP
Every hook receives the edge and the rung being left, plus
.BR VIGILANCE_EDGE ,
.BR VIGILANCE_FROM ,
.B VIGILANCE_SOURCE
(who asked),
.B VIGILANCE_STATE_DIR
and two the runner states so no hook has to restate them:
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_INTENT
.BR dark ", " lit " or " none:
the darkness this edge implies. A hook honouring it cannot fall through a
stale case when the ladder grows a rung.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_KIND
.BR act ", " verify ", " report ", " due ", " block " or " audit,
which question is being asked, so ONE plugin can serve several kinds.
.PP
vigilance ships plugins under
.I libexec/
but enables none: which ones run is the integrator's policy.
.SS WHO ASKED: the VIGILANCE_SOURCE vocabulary
Every crossing records it
.RB ( "cross lock: open \-> lock src=idle" ),
so the log can answer the question a human actually asks of it: why did my
screen go dark, and why did it come back. A crossing nobody labelled records
.BR unset ,
which is deliberately distinguishable from a labelled one, and a value with a
character outside
.B [a\-z0\-9\-]
records
.B invalid
rather than being written through: a newline in it would split one record in
two and break the one\-record\-one\-line contract the audit tier parses by.
.PP
THE VOCABULARY IS OPEN. vigilant itself never branches on the value; only
hooks do, and a hook is the only thing that knows what a source means. So an
integrator wiring a new gesture gives it a name of their own and nothing here
needs to learn it. What follows is what the SHIPPED callers set:
.TP
.B idle
the idle timer's own lock, at its first threshold. The one source
.I phantom-guard
debounces, and the only one it may: gating any other would be a screen that
does not lock.
.TP
.B blank
the idle timer's second threshold, which takes the machine to
.BR sleep .
Deliberately NOT
.BR idle :
from
.B open
that traversal crosses the lock edge too, and labelling it
.B idle
would newly subject it to the debounce, which could leave the box neither
asleep nor locked.
.TP
.B input
the idle timer's resume arm, the ascent that gives a woken box its screen back.
.TP
.B logind
a
.B Session.Lock
on the bus. A LID CLOSE ARRIVES THIS WAY and cannot be told apart: logind
applies its own
.B HandleLidSwitch
policy and emits the same signal, so there is nothing for the trigger to
distinguish.
.TP
.B manual
a human asking, by keybind or by hand.
.TP
.B suspend
the system sleep unit, before S3.
.TP
.B resume
the resume unit, after it.
.TP
.B locker\-exit
the locker's unit stopping, which is how an unlock is DETECTED: nothing
watches for one. It says the locker stopped, whichever way it stopped, so a
crash and a human typing their password read alike here.
.TP
.B inhibit\-bound
the idle inhibit bound forcing a lock. See
.B THE IDLE INHIBIT BOUND
above.
.TP
.B rescue
the panic key.
.SS Hooks are time-bounded
Hooks run in LEXICAL order, and the lock provider is not first, so a hook that
hangs blocks every later hook on its edge: including the one that locks the
screen. Under
.I lock-on-sleep.service
that removes the guarantee entirely: systemd kills the unit at its 25s timeout
and nothing can veto a suspend, so the box sleeps UNLOCKED.
.PP
Each hook therefore runs under
.BR timeout (1)
with a SIGKILL backstop, defaulting to 10 seconds and set by
.BR VIGILANCE_HOOK_TIMEOUT
.RI ( 0
disables it). A hook killed this way is logged as
.B HOOK TIMED OUT
and counts as a failure. The same bound applies to
.I alert.d
hooks, which are reached from a hook failure: a notifier that hangs would
otherwise block the very edge whose failure raised it.
.PP
The default is generous because it bounds a HANG, not an SLA: a healthy
.BR ddcutil (1)
pass over three displays takes seconds. It cannot reach a locker started with
.BR systemd\-run (1),
which is a sibling unit rather than a child; killing a foreground locker
mid\-lock would leave a frozen abandoned session lock, which is why the
architecture forbids running one as a child. If
.BR timeout (1)
is absent the bound is silently gone, so
.B vigilant report
warns.
.PP
The
.I audit.d
tier has its OWN, larger budget,
.BR VIGILANCE_AUDIT_TIMEOUT " (60s)."
An audit source reads an external event log: the shipped one runs
.BR journalctl (1)
over a day of records, and legitimately takes longer than anything on the
lock path may. Sharing the edge bound would turn a healthy slow read into
.B "audit BROKEN"
and retire the forensic tier by crying wolf. Nothing waits on it, so latency is
not the property being protected there; only the hang is.
.SS The edge budget
.B vigilant report
compares the hooks wired on the
.B lock
edge against the time
.I lock-on-sleep.service
allows, because those are two numbers in two files that must relate and nothing
else relates them.
.PP
The number that matters is not the total, it is the time before the screen is
LOCKED. Hooks run in lexical order and
.I block.d
runs before all of them, so every hook ordered ahead of the lock provider can
delay the lock itself; anything in
.IR report.d " or " verify.d
runs after the screen is already locked and risks only the unit being killed. So
.B "(block + act)"
is reported as the critical span and the total separately.
.PP
It WARNS rather than fails: what is wrong is the headroom, not the state, and a
correct machine turned red is how a report stops being read. The remedies are to
order the provider first, lower
.BR VIGILANCE_HOOK_TIMEOUT ,
or drop a hook.
.SS Monitor power, and why only standby
The
.I ddc\-monitor
plugin powers displays down over DDC/MCCS VCP feature D6. It uses ONLY
.B 02
(DPMS standby), and only on a monitor that advertises it.
.PP
D6 is not a scale of "more off", it is a set of states with different WAKE
CHANNELS. 02 is shallow: the scaler keeps listening and
.B 01
brings the panel back. 03, 04 and 05 are suspend, full off, and "turn off
display"; none of them promises the monitor is still answering I2C afterwards,
and if it is not there is NO WAY BACK over DDC.
.PP
Observed: a Dell AW2725Q advertising 01/04/05 and no 02 was driven into an off
state, stopped answering DDC entirely, and needed a power cycle at the monitor.
A panel offering no shallow standby therefore has no DDC\-recoverable off
state, and the absence of 02 is itself the signal. Such a monitor is left on
and the reason is reported; compositor\-side true black covers it, which on
OLED genuinely is off.
.PP
.B VIGILANCE_DDC_DEEP_OFF=1
permits 03/04/05 on panels lacking 02, for an operator who has VERIFIED on their
own hardware that the monitor still answers DDC when off. It never overrides 02
where that exists.
.PP
A monitor that WAS driven dark and then disappears from
.BR ddcutil (1)
detect is reported as drift rather than absence: a dark panel that stops
answering DDC is reclassified as an invalid display, which would otherwise make
it silently invisible to the ascent that should have woken it.
.SH TESTING
Two substrates run the same scenarios
.RB ( "sh test/run" " and " "sh test/vm/run" ),
and the stub one stubs ACTUATORS but never the trust root: a stubbed
.BR systemctl (1)
lies, and a lying stub is how a green test coexists with a broken box.
.PP
.B sh test/mutate
is the third tier and asks whether the guards BITE. Each record in
.I test/mutants.rec
removes one guard and names the tests that must notice; a guard nothing kills
is decorative. The driver asserts the mutation LANDED, that the result still
PARSES, and that the named tests were green beforehand, because every failure
of this discipline here has been in the instrument rather than the subject.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Forty-one knobs, and an audit found seventy percent of them documented
nowhere. The extension points existed and were not discoverable, which for a
package whose whole claim is "ship the 80 percent, dial in the rest" is the
gap that matters most.
.PP
The five groups below are not cosmetic. Which group a knob is in says who is
allowed to set it, and the last two are the ones people get wrong: a hook
INPUT set by hand lies to the hook, and a PROBE OVERRIDE set in production
makes a tier measure a fixture instead of the machine.
.SS Placement: where vigilance keeps things
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_HOOK_ROOT
user hook tree (default ~/.config/vigilance/hooks)
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_MACHINE_HOOKS
machine hook tree (default /etc/vigilance/hooks)
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_LOG
the edge log the audit tier parses
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_RESCUE_LOG
where `rescue` writes its transcript
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_RUN_DIR
runtime state; the depth record lives here
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_RUNTIME_BASE
per-uid runtime root (default /run/user)
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_HOOK_PATH
the PATH every hook is given
.SS Mechanism: the dials for a different stack
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_LOCKER
locker process name (default swaylock). The NAME, because
.B locker\-up
and
.B lock\-blank
look the process up by it; its argv and its unit type are the two knobs below,
which is the difference between a documented extension point and a name the
code ignores.
.IP
ONLY THE FIRST 15 BYTES ARE SIGNIFICANT, and that is the kernel's limit rather
than this package's: a process name is read from
.BR /proc/<pid>/comm ,
which holds 15 bytes and a terminator. So
.B xfce4\-screensaver
(17) and
.B gnome\-screensaver
(17) are matched on
.B xfce4\-screensav
and
.BR gnome\-screensave ,
and two lockers agreeing in their first 15 bytes cannot be told apart. Matching
is LITERAL, so a name is never read as a pattern.
.IP
Until 2026\-10\-01 the lookup was
.BR "pgrep \-x" ,
which was wrong in both directions for a name given here. An exact match on
more than 15 bytes can never succeed, so a correctly locked box reported no
locker and alerted on it once a minute; and the pattern is an ERE, so a name
containing
.B .*
matched every process, which reported a secured session with nothing locking
and, in
.BR lock\-blank 's
signal, would have signalled the whole machine.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_LOCKER_ARGV
extra arguments for it (default \fB\-f\fR, swaylock's \fB\-\-daemonize\fR).
Hardcoded until 2026\-09\-29, which made
.B VIGILANCE_LOCKER
a half promise: \fBi3lock\fR resolved, passed the existence check and was then
handed a flag it does not have, so the unit failed and the screen did not
lock: on the one edge where that matters. An X11 box is
.B "VIGILANCE_LOCKER=i3lock VIGILANCE_LOCKER_ARGV=\-n"
with the type below.
.IP
The integrator's
.B vigilance\-lock\-argv
helper is asked separately and is passed the locker name, because ITS output is
locker\-specific too: the shipped one emits swaylock's \fB\-C <conf>\fR, and
before it was told which locker it was answering for, substituting one produced
\fBi3lock \-C .../swaylock.conf\fR.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_LOCKER_TYPE
the transient unit's systemd
.B Type
(default \fBforking\fR). It asserts that the locker DETACHES once the screen is
covered. A foreground locker (\fBxsecurelock\fR, \fBi3lock \-n\fR) under
\fBforking\fR leaves
.B systemd\-run
waiting for a fork that never comes, and the provider then reports failure about
a screen that is locked; such a locker wants \fBsimple\fR.
.IP
AND \fBsimple\fR ESTABLISHES NOTHING, which is the hazard in the other
direction.
.B systemd\-run
returns the instant the unit is started, so a locker that dies in milliseconds
was reported as a successful lock: measured in the guest, where the crossing
returned 0 with no locker anywhere. The provider therefore confirms the unit
survived (see
.BR VIGILANCE_LOCK_CONFIRM ),
which costs nothing under \fBforking\fR because
.B systemd\-run
blocked until the fork.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_LOCKER_UNSETENV
names to REMOVE from the locker unit's environment, space separated. SETTING one
needs no knob: the unit inherits the user manager's environment, so an
integrator imports it there, which is the advice this page already gives for
.BR WAYLAND_DISPLAY .
Removing one for a single unit had no mechanism at all, and that asymmetry is
why this exists.
.IP
AN X11 LOCKER NEEDS IT. \fBi3lock\fR checks
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
and refuses whatever
.B DISPLAY
says, so on any box that has run a Wayland session, and such a box is told to
import that variable into its manager, the X11 example above could not start at
all. \fBVIGILANCE_LOCKER_UNSETENV=WAYLAND_DISPLAY\fR is the remaining piece.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_LOCK_CONFIRM
seconds to keep watching a non\-forking locker after its unit starts
(default 2).
.IP
EVERY TYPE IS SAMPLED ONCE, immediately, and this knob only governs how long
a non\-forking one is watched after that. An earlier version of this page said
\fBforking\fR was "already waited for" and so needed no check at all, which
is FALSE: measured, a script that exits 0 without forking gives
.B systemd\-run
rc=0 and a unit that is already \fBinactive\fR, so a locker which delegates and
exits was reported as a successful lock under the DEFAULT type. The two cases
differ immediately, so the first sample costs one
.B systemctl show
and nothing on the lock path worth counting; only the WATCHING is type\-gated,
because under \fBsimple\fR a locker can be running at that instant and gone a
moment later.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_IDLE_PROCESS
idle daemon; EMPTY means this box has none
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_IDLE_UNIT
unit supervising it; EMPTY means none
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_LOCK_UNIT
transient unit the lock provider starts
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_GREETER_USER
uid `plan` judges reachability for
.PP
THE UNIT SET IS DECLARED, NOT ASSUMED. The five below are what
.B report
checks under
.BR machinery ,
and each defaults to the shipped name, so a box wired the standard way needs
none of them. EMPTY means "this box declares none", exactly as
.B VIGILANCE_IDLE_PROCESS
already works: an integration that wires a subset should say so rather than
collect a permanent FAIL about a machine that is working.
.PP
THE CHECK RUNS BOTH WAYS. A declared unit that is missing is a FAIL, as it
always was; a unit DECLARED NONE that is nonetheless installed is a WARN naming
it, because otherwise declaring a unit absent would be the way to switch a real
finding off.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_LOGIND_UNIT
the Session.Lock listener (\fB\-\-user\fR, must be active)
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_SLEEP_UNIT
the suspend guarantee (SYSTEM, must be enabled)
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_RESUME_UNIT
the resume path (SYSTEM, must be enabled)
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_ENFORCE_UNIT
the supervision pass timer (\fB\-\-user\fR, must be active)
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_AUDIT_UNIT
the forensic pass timer (\fB\-\-user\fR, must be active)
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_SCREEN_GRAB
the command that captures a frame, writing an image to STDOUT and nothing
else. Defaults to
.B grim \-c \-s 0.05 \-
on Wayland and
.B import \-silent \-window root \-resize 5%
on X11, chosen by which tool exists and which display is present. Supply it
where neither is the right tool: a KDE Plasma session has neither and reaches
for spectacle. This is NOT a probe override and does not belong with them: only
the frame's SOURCE changes, while the mean, the peak, the alpha handling and
the whole-surface rule stay shared, so the statistic cannot drift with the
platform. The CURSOR is the grabber's responsibility and the peak reading rests
on it: \fBgrim \-c\fR composites the pointer, \fBimport\fR has no equivalent
and is blind to one, and a supplied grabber inherits that.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_SCREENSAVER_NAME
the D-Bus name a FOREIGN screensaver answers on, default
.BR org.freedesktop.ScreenSaver .
At rung
.B open
report asks it whether the screen is active, which is the only way this package
can notice a locker it neither started nor was told about.
.B SET THIS ON A DESKTOP WHOSE SCREENSAVER ANSWERS ON ITS OWN NAME:
xfce4-screensaver owns
.B org.xfce.ScreenSaver
and implements only that interface, so the default finds nothing there. The
object path and the interface both FOLLOW the name given here, with the
freedesktop interface tried first, so one setting is enough and a conforming
implementation is unaffected. Measured against a real xfce4-screensaver in the
guest, where setting the name alone was NOT enough until the interface followed
it too. Three further things about the probe are deliberate. It is asked only
where the name is
.BR ACQUIRED ,
never where it is merely activatable, because calling an unowned activatable
name makes D\-Bus START that daemon and vigilance must not spawn a rival locker
while asking whether one exists. Nothing owning the name reports
.BR "cannot tell" ,
never
.BR no .
And a disagreement is a WARN rather than a FAIL, because a desktop that owns
its own locking will raise its screensaver with this ladder correctly at
.BR open ,
and a report that is permanently non-zero is one nobody reads.
.SS cursor.d: is the POINTER auto-hidden
.PP
A CROSS\-CUTTING tier, like
.IR idle.d " and " audit.d ,
because the pointer belongs to the SEAT rather than to any edge. The runner asks
it in one place, the
.B cursor
section of
.BR report ,
and only where the answer matters: a box whose panel is darkened by PAINTING
BLACK rather than by a backlight or a monitor power state. There the pixels are
off only while nothing brighter appears, and a notification brings the pointer
up. Hiding the cursor once when the locker fires does not survive that; the
mechanism has to be a compositor\-level AUTO\-hide.
.PP
THE CONTRACT, and the third value is the point of having a tier at all:
.TP
.B 0
something auto\-hides the pointer. Name it on stdout; the report quotes it.
.TP
.B 1
this platform is RECOGNISED and the mechanism is ABSENT. A real no.
.TP
.B 78
cannot tell here, which is NOT the same as no and is the default answer.
.PP
ONE YES IS ENOUGH and one no is not decisive: two mechanisms may be available
with only one in use, so a single hook answering 0 settles it while a single 1
must not overrule another that would have said otherwise.
.PP
WHY THIS IS A TIER AND NOT A SETTING. There is no platform\-neutral way to
detect it: no Wayland protocol advertises cursor\-hide state, sway's
.B hide_cursor
is a config directive rather than a runtime query, wayfire is answerable only by
its plugin being MAPPED into the compositor, and X11 only by a helper process
being alive. A knob would have been a promise; a hook that returns 0 is a
measurement, and the shipped
.B cursor\-autohide
answers for wayfire and for X11 helpers and declines elsewhere.
.SS Policy and bounds
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_HOOK_TIMEOUT
per-hook bound, seconds (default 10)
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_AUDIT_TIMEOUT
the audit tier's larger bound (default 60)
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_GRACE
slack before an edge counts as overdue
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_INHIBIT_REPORT
how long a held idle inhibitor may defer an idle-anchored deadline before that
is reported (default 10800); 0 never reports
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_INHIBIT_FORCE
how long before the session is secured anyway (default 14400); 0 never acts
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_ALERT_COOLDOWN
repeat-alert suppression; 0 disables
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_AUDIT_WINDOW
how far an event may sit from its edge
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_LOCK_ACTIVE_FILE
PROBE OVERRIDE, tests only. A path whose CONTENT stands in for the lock unit's
ActiveState (absent means inactive), so the provider's lost\-race branch can be
exercised without a user systemd bus. Setting it in production makes the
provider believe a file instead of the machine.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_LOCK_SETTLE
how long the lock provider waits for a concurrently\-started locker to finish
coming up, seconds (default 5). Only reached when our own start was refused
because the unit already exists; a unit that is inactive rather than
activating is not waited for at all.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_CROSS_WAIT
how long a second crossing waits for the first, seconds (default 2). One
crossing runs at a time; a request that arrives while another is in flight
waits, then re\-reads the depth and usually finds the work already done. On
expiry it crosses ANYWAY and says so in the log: a mutex on the lock path must
never be able to prevent a lock. 0 disables the wait, which is what
.B rescue
uses.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_CROSS_TOKEN
set by vigilant, never by hand. Carries the crossing lock's holder to its own
hooks so a hook that calls back into
.B "vigilant go"
is not made to wait for its own ancestor.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_CROSSING_MAX
how long an in\-flight crossing stays believable, seconds (default 120). The
standing recheck skips a machine that is mid\-crossing, because the depth is
committed before the act tier and the record is ahead of the machine on
purpose. This caps how long a marker left behind by a killed vigilant can
suppress that check; the writer being alive is the primary test.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_PERIPHERAL_EVERY
how often a PERIPHERAL verifier re\-checks under the standing recheck, seconds
(default 3600). Cadence follows consequence, not uniformity: a check that
answers "is the session secured" earns a minute, and one that answers "is the
keyboard's RGB still off" does not. Measured before this existed: the recheck
ran the whole verify tier every minute at 2.0s a pass, 48 minutes of work a
day on an idle machine, of which one second was a single ddcutil probe, and
in the entire log history the peripheral verifiers had reported drift exactly
never.
.IP
WHAT MAKES A SLOW CADENCE SAFE is that the recheck forces a FULL verify on the
first pass after the rung CHANGES, which is when drift is actually introduced:
the keyboard\-backlight drift appeared 14 seconds after a crossing. The hourly
pass is a backstop for the case nobody thought of, not the primary mechanism.
An explicit
.B vigilant verify
never throttles, including the one
.B lock\-on\-sleep
runs before a suspend.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_RECHECK
set by the runner, never by hand: present only while the standing recheck is
verifying, which is the one context in which a hook may skip as not due (exit
75).
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_RECHECK_GRACE
how long after entering a rung the transition\-triggered full verify is still
merely PENDING rather than missing, seconds (default 300). Read by
.B report
alone, for its
.B cadence
section: the supervision timer owns the real period and the integrator owns the
timer, so a box wired with a 15\-minute pass is not faulty for having gone a
minute without verifying. Raise it to match a slower timer. The section states
the fact either way; this only decides whether it is reported as pending or as
a warning.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_IDLE_PHASE
set by the runner, never by hand: `settle` tells an
.B idle.d
source to take a reading and remember it WITHOUT answering. The supervision pass
reads the clock before any hook runs and settles afterwards, so the window a
source judges is the one in which vigilance did nothing. That matters because
the pass generates traffic on the devices a counter clock watches: a wired
verify hook queries a keyboard over raw HID and it answers because it was asked,
which was measured at 6 URBs three seconds after every pass and pinned the clock
at one interval for ever. A source that ignores this phase simply answers twice
and the extra answer is discarded.
.IP
A SOURCE THAT REPORTS NO CEILING IS TAKEN AT ITS WORD, and that is a contract
rather than an oversight. The field is an optional extension that exists
because a NUMBER is not evidence the deadline is OBSERVABLE: a clock that
resets every 74 seconds can never witness 480. Omitting it claims the answer
stands on its own. Until a second source existed, silence here meant
"unimplemented" and "unbounded" indistinguishably.
.IP
AND ALMOST EVERY SOURCE HAS A CAP, which is worth saying because the second one
looked like the exception and was not.
.B x11\-idle
asks the X server, so its answer is continuous rather than sampled, and
XScreenSaverQueryInfo reports idle time WITHIN the current screensaver state, so
the counter RESTARTS when the saver activates. Measured against a live server:
306s, then 2s three seconds later. So it reports the server's own screensaver
timeout as its ceiling, and reports none only when that timeout is 0. The cap is
STRUCTURAL rather than accumulated, so it reports
.B age
equal to it: report's "the clock is too young to have seen that yet" exemption
must not excuse a hard limit.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_IDLE_MAXGAP
how long a gap between idle samples may be before the clock stops trusting it,
seconds (default 300). An idle counter measures the interval since it last
changed, so a stretch when nothing SAMPLED looks identical to a quiet seat. A
gap over this bound re\-anchors the clock, reports activity (the safe direction
for a clock that gates an alert) and drops the ceiling it cannot vouch for.
Measured on a live box before this existed: a ceiling of 67 hours on a clock
whose counter was in fact moving every 25 seconds.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_SLEEP_BUDGET
the suspend unit's TimeoutStartSec, for report
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_INITIAL_DEPTH
rung a session starts at (a greeter: lock)
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_DDC_DEEP_OFF
allow DDC power states beyond standby
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_LOCK_BLANK
auto|always|never for the black lock surface
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_HEARTBEAT_MARK
argv marker proving the idle timer arms a heartbeat (default idle\-tick)
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_WATCHDOG_QUIET
silence before the idle timer is suspect. Counted only over time the
supervision pass was actually RUNNING, so a suspend does not accumulate it
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_WATCHDOG_MAXGAP
how long a gap between two supervision passes means we were not watching, so
the silence window re-anchors (default 300)
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_PHANTOM_COOLDOWN
the phantom-relock guard's window
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_AUDIT_SINCE
how far back an audit source reads
.SS Passed TO hooks by the runner, never set by an operator
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_EDGE
the edge being crossed
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_KIND
act|verify|report|due|block|audit|idle|watchdog
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_FROM
the rung being left
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_INTENT
dark|lit|none for this edge
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_SOURCE
who asked; see
.B WHO ASKED
below for the vocabulary
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_STATE_DIR
a private directory for that hook
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_ALERT_KIND
the alert kind, to an alert.d hook
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_ALERT_MSG
the alert text, to an alert.d hook
.SS Probe overrides: these exist so a tier measures a FIXTURE
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_SYS_BACKLIGHT
sysfs backlight root
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_SYS_LEDS
sysfs leds root
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_SYS_DRM
sysfs drm root
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_SYS_INPUT
sysfs input root
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_SWITCH_DEVICES
the devices udev tags power-switch, for the triggers section
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_BLOCK_INHIBITED
logind's BlockInhibited string, for the triggers section
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_PROC_INTERRUPTS
the interrupts file
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_UPTIME_FILE
the uptime file
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_SCREEN_LUMA
short-circuit the screen capture, supplying the MEAN luminance
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_SCREEN_PEAK
and the PEAK, which is a separate question: the mean asks whether the whole
surface is emitting and the peak asks whether ANYTHING is. Setting the mean
alone leaves the peak unknown, which is reported as such rather than as a pass,
so a fixture cannot satisfy the bright-spot check by saying nothing about it.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_LOCKER_UP
1|0 instead of looking for the locker
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_SCREENSAVER
active|idle|anything else, instead of asking the bus. The third value is the
cannot\-tell answer, and it is the one a box with no rival gives, so it is the
default behaviour rather than an error case. Here and not with the mechanism
dials because it replaces the measurement outright: a tier running under it is
measuring a fixture, which is what makes the foreign\-locker outcome reachable
without installing a rival desktop.
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_SESSION
the logind session id
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_IDLE_CMDLINE
a file standing in for /proc/<pid>/cmdline
.TP
.B VIGILANCE_IDLE_STARTED
seconds the idle timer has been running, standing in for the
.B ps
read. It is a CEILING on idle time: a restarted timer re-arms every timeout, so
until it has been up as long as a deadline that deadline is not late
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Ok, including a no-op.
.TP
.B 78
NOT APPLICABLE: a hook saying "there is nothing here for me to do". Distinct
from 0 on purpose: exit 0 means BOTH "I did the work" and "not applicable",
so nothing downstream can tell a tier where every hook CONFIRMED from one where
every hook DECLINED. Measured consequence: on a desktop whose monitor has no
DPMS standby and which has no sysfs backlight, both screen verifiers quietly
declined and
.B "verify sleep"
reported OK: the screen was checked by nothing while the tier that exists to
catch that reported green.
.IP
The runner counts these apart from success and failure.
.B verify
names them ("ok (3 checked, 1 n/a: panel-backlight)") and reports an edge where
EVERY hook declined as
.BR "NOTHING CHECKED" ,
which is a FAIL: the state is unknown rather than good. An n/a raises no alert
and does not degrade a crossing.
.IP
.B vigilant verify
ITSELF exits 78 when the edge has NO verify hooks at all. That closes the same
hole one level up: the contract was honoured between a hook and the runner and
broken between the runner and its caller, so every wired hook declining was a
FAIL while none being wired was a pass. Identical state, opposite verdict.
.IP
It is load-bearing rather than tidy.
.I lock\-on\-sleep.service
runs
.B "vigilant verify lock"
as
.B ExecStartPost
and that is the suspend\-time lock guarantee; systemd reads the STATUS and
cannot read the sentence. With 0 there, emptying
.I lock.verify.d
made the unit report success and the box suspend with its lock verified by
nothing. 78 is distinct from failure, so a caller asking "was this verified"
learns the truth while
.B report
renders it WARN and still exits 0.
.IP
Using it is OPTIONAL. A hook that exits 0 is counted as having checked, exactly
as before, so no existing integration changes behaviour.
.TP
.B 1
Crossed, but one or more hooks failed (degraded, but it moved).
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
Refused: a precondition was not met and nothing happened.
.TP
.B 75
NOT DUE: a hook saying "I checked recently". Read from a hook by the runner
and never returned by
.BR vigilant ,
and distinct from both of its neighbours for the same reason 78 is distinct from
0: "nothing is wired to look" is a failure, while "everything was checked inside
the hour" is the machine being COVERED, and collapsing those would make a fully
throttled tier read as a verify nobody performed. In the safe\-looking
direction.
.IP
OFFERED ONLY TO THE STANDING RECHECK, through
.BR VIGILANCE_RECHECK .
An explicit
.B vigilant verify
never throttles, including the one
.B lock\-on\-sleep.service
runs before a suspend, because "I looked an hour ago" is not an answer to "is
the session secured right now".
.B hook_lib
supplies
.B hook_throttle
for it; see
.B VIGILANCE_PERIPHERAL_EVERY
for the cadence and the measurements behind it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR /vigilance/depth
The recorded rung, and the authoritative state. It lives in the runtime dir
because that survives suspend and hibernate but NOT a reboot, which is exactly
right: a boot means the machine is at the top of the ladder.
.IP
When
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is unset: a non\-interactive ssh, cron,
.BR su (1):
vigilant looks for
.IR /run/user/ <uid>,
which logind maintains for any session of that uid, so the real record is still
found. Failing that it uses a PER\-UID directory under
.BR $TMPDIR ,
never a shared name in a world\-writable one, and
.B vigilant report
warns: nothing else writes there, so every rung would read as
.B open
regardless of where the machine actually is.
.TP
.I systemd/vigilance-logind.service
The --user Session.Lock listener (install with
.BR "setup.sh service" ).
.TP
.I systemd/lock-on-sleep.service
SYSTEM suspend-lock unit (Before=sleep.target); needs root.
.TP
.I systemd/vigilance-resume.service
SYSTEM resume unit (After=suspend.target); needs root.
.TP
.I systemd/vigilance-enforce.timer
The supervision loop, every minute. It REPORTS and never acts: it re\-asks
whether the machine still matches the rung it claims, and whether any deadline
has passed. Forcing is retired; see "What forcing was, and why it is gone".
.TP
.I systemd/vigilance-audit.timer
The forensic pass, daily and
.BR Persistent= ,
so a box that was asleep at the scheduled time still runs it on waking.
.TP
.I systemd/vigilance-idle.service
Keeps the idle timer alive. Placed but never enabled against a target: only the
COMPOSITOR knows when a display exists to connect to, so it starts this from its
own autostart.
.TP
.I udev/99-vigilance.rules
Grants the
.B vigilant
group write on exactly the nodes the peripheral hooks drive. Needs root.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/vigilance.log
vigilant's own record of every crossing, block and alert.
.SH THE vigilant GROUP
The peripheral hooks drive backlights and LEDs through
.BR brightnessctl ,
and those nodes are root-owned. Without
.I udev/99-vigilance.rules
and a
.B vigilant
group containing every user that runs vigilance (the greeter user included, if
greeter coverage is on), those writes are DENIED and the hooks no-op SILENTLY:
.B hook_dark
and
.B hook_lit
swallow the failure by design, so vigilant logs clean crossings while the
hardware never moves.
.PP
Deliberately not the
.B input
group, which also grants raw read on
.IR /dev/input/event* ,
i.e. every keystroke.
.B "setup.sh check"
verifies the group and the membership.
.SH WHAT CANNOT BE GUARANTEED
NOTHING CAN VETO A SUSPEND. A unit ordered
.B Before=sleep.target
delays the transition while it runs, and a delay inhibitor delays it too
.RB ( InhibitDelayMaxSec ),
but when either one fails or times out, logind proceeds. There is no supported
way to refuse a suspend outright, and a suite that could refuse one could strand
a laptop on a critical battery.
.PP
So the suspend lock's goal is not prevention: it is that sleeping unlocked
becomes LOUD and RECORDED instead of a green light.
.I lock-on-sleep.service
runs
.B "vigilant verify lock"
after crossing, so that "the unit succeeded" is a claim about the SESSION rather
than merely about the hooks, and the
.I audit
tier reconciles it afterwards either way.
.SH RUNTIME DEPENDENCIES
.BR swaylock ", " swayidle ", " wlopm ", " ddcutil ", " brightnessctl ,
each used only by a plugin and each degrading gracefully when absent. Run
.B "setup.sh check"
to see which are present and what each one's absence costs.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR swaylock (1),
.BR swayidle (1),
.BR systemd.service (5)
